export const html = "<article class=\"research-article\">\n<header class=\"research-header\"><div class=\"editorial-kicker\">Market review / USDE short interest</div>\n<h1>How much StablecoinX stock was sold short in September 2026?</h1>\n<p class=\"article-byline\">By <a href=\"/about/\">Qorban Ferrell · @Degenerate_DeFi</a><span>Published <time datetime=\"2026-10-09\">9 October 2026</time></span></p>\n<p class=\"research-cutoff\">Short interest through the September 30, 2026 settlement date · Trading from June 26 through the October 8 close · Sources read October 9, 2026 UTC</p></header>\n<nav class=\"research-contents\" aria-label=\"In this article\"><span>In this review</span><a href=\"#short-interest-reached-281-million-shares-on-september-30\">Short interest</a><a href=\"#short-sellers-held-less-than-half-a-days-volume\">Days to cover</a><a href=\"#daily-short-sale-volume-measures-trading\">Daily short volume</a><a href=\"#daily-prices-suggest-the-short-sale-price-test-covered-more-than-half-of-usdes-sessions\">Price test</a><a href=\"#reproduce-the-numbers\">Download data</a></nav>\n<p class=\"research-opening\">Short sellers held 2,810,986 shares of USDE, the Class A stock of StablecoinX Inc. on Nasdaq, on September 30, 2026, the latest settlement date in FINRA's data when this review last checked it, at 20:37 UTC on October 9. That was 14.7% of the roughly 19.06 million Class A shares that the company's resale prospectus counted as freely tradable on August 28. Short interest rose by 1,231,508 shares, or 78.0%, from September 15.</p>\n<p>Short interest is the number of shares sold short and not yet bought back. Member firms report it to FINRA, the self-regulatory body for US broker-dealers, for two settlement dates a month, one in the middle and one at the end. A short sale counts once it has settled, one business day after the trade.</p>\n<p>The short position was small next to USDE's trading. In the 11 sessions to September 30 the stock traded an average of 6.96 million shares a day, so short sellers held 0.40 days of volume, a ratio called days to cover. From its first session on June 26 to October 8, USDE traded 380.8 million shares, 20 times its freely tradable shares.</p>\n<p>FINRA's daily short sale volume, a separate measure that some websites present as short interest, counts the share volume of trades. Of the 157.0 million USDE shares traded in regular hours and reported to FINRA's trade reporting facilities, the systems that record trades made away from exchanges, 57.8% were marked short. A short sale counts there even if the seller buys the shares back the same day, so the 57.8% measures trading.</p>\n<p>USDE closed at $6.12 on September 15, reached a peak close of $17.25 on September 25 and finished September at $16.08. FINRA's September 30 figures, published on October 9, show that aggregate short interest increased across that period. The two snapshots do not reveal when individual positions were opened or closed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"short-interest-reached-281-million-shares-on-september-30\">Short interest reached 2.81 million shares on September 30</h2>\n<p>FINRA collects short positions from its member firms in every exchange-listed and over-the-counter stock under its Rule 4560. Firms report the positions they hold at the middle and the end of each month, and FINRA publishes the totals on the seventh business day after each settlement date. Its <a href=\"https://www.finra.org/finra-data/browse-catalog/equity-short-interest\">short interest data</a> for USDE starts at June 30, the first settlement date after the stock's first session.</p>\n<div class=\"research-table\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Research data table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"col\">Settlement date</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Short interest, shares</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Change</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Share of freely tradable shares</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">USDE close</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Days to cover</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>June 30</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">12,599</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+12,599</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.07%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">$2.75</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.04</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>July 15</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">277,768</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+265,169</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.46%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">$1.82</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.04</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>July 31</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">261,864</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−15,904</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.37%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">$1.57</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.42</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>August 14</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">196,109</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−65,755</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.03%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">$2.93</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.45</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>August 31</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1,232,013</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+1,035,904</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">6.46%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">$8.87</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.08</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>September 15</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1,579,478</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+347,465</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">8.29%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">$6.12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.43</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>September 30</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2,810,986</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+1,231,508</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">14.75%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">$16.08</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.40</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>This review divides by the prospectus's approximate count of 19,063,653 freely tradable shares on August 28 at every date. From 4 pm on September 14 the resale prospectus also registered for sale the 3,621,132 Class A shares of Ethena OpCo Ltd, an Ethena company, which the prospectus says carry no contractual lock-up. Against those and the freely tradable shares, 22,684,785 in all, the September 30 short interest came to 12.39%. Against all 24,029,375 Class A shares it came to 11.70%. At the $16.08 close it was worth $45.20 million.</p>\n<figure class=\"research-figure\"><div class=\"research-chart\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"USDE daily close and FINRA short interest, two panels\"><img width=\"800\" height=\"466\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Two panels on one time axis, June 26 to October 8, 2026. USDE closed at $3.70 on June 26, fell to a low close of $1.43 on July 24 and rose to $17.25 on September 25 before closing at $12.79 on October 8. Short interest at the seven settlement dates was 12,599, 277,768, 261,864, 196,109, 1,232,013, 1,579,478 and 2,810,986 shares.\" src=\"/research-assets/stablecoinx-short-interest-2026-10-08/chart_short_interest.svg\" /></div><figcaption>Short interest from FINRA's consolidated short interest data. Daily closes from S&amp;P Global Market Intelligence through StockAnalysis. · <a href=\"/research-assets/stablecoinx-short-interest-2026-10-08/chart_short_interest.svg\">Open full chart ↗</a></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Short interest also rose sharply in the second half of August, when short interest grew by 1,035,904 shares and USDE's close rose from $2.93 to $8.87. Short interest in the warrants, USDEW, was 7,731 warrants on September 30, 0.07% of the 11.5 million public warrants.</p>\n<h2 id=\"short-sellers-held-less-than-half-a-days-volume\">Short sellers held less than half a day's volume</h2>\n<p>FINRA listed days to cover as 1 for every date, the value its data shows for anything at or below one day. Divided by the average daily volume in S&amp;P Global's data over each reporting cycle, from the session after one settlement date to the next, USDE's short interest never reached half a day's trading. FINRA's own average daily volume came within 4% of S&amp;P Global's in every cycle but the first. FINRA averages over every trading day in a cycle, and USDE traded on three of the ten in its first.</p>\n<p>Trading was heavy for a stock with 19.06 million freely tradable shares. On August 21 alone USDE traded 99.0 million shares, 5.2 times the freely tradable shares, as its close rose 85% from $4.00 to $7.41. That one session lifted the average for the cycle to August 31 to 15.0 million shares a day, which is why days to cover on that date was 0.08. Without it the cycle averaged 6.61 million shares a day, and days to cover would have been 0.19.</p>\n<h2 id=\"daily-short-sale-volume-measures-trading\">Daily short sale volume measures trading</h2>\n<p>FINRA's <a href=\"https://www.finra.org/finra-data/browse-catalog/short-sale-volume-data/daily-short-sale-volume-files\">daily short sale volume files</a> give, for each stock, the volume of regular-hours trades reported to its trade reporting facilities and the part of it marked short. Trades executed on an exchange are not in them, and exchanges publish their own short sale volume. For USDE the files covered 157.0 million shares over the 73 sessions, 41.2% of the 380.8 million shares in S&amp;P Global's daily volume.</p>\n<div class=\"research-table\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Research data table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"col\">FINRA daily files, June 26 to October 8</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Shares traded, regular hours, reported to FINRA</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">156,995,182</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Of which marked short</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">90,743,186</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short volume marked short exempt</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">6,969,314</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Share marked short</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">57.8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Median session</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">54.2%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lowest session</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">13.7%, on August 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Highest session</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">79.6%, on June 26</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sessions over half</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">47 of 73</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The files count only trades published on the consolidated tape, the public feed of trades, according to a <a href=\"https://www.finra.org/rules-guidance/notices/information-notice-051019\">FINRA notice of May 2019</a>. When a firm fills a customer's sale by first selling short from its own account to another firm and then buying the customer's shares at the same price, only the short sale appears in them, which can make the share marked short look high.</p>\n<p>Short interest counts the positions open at a settlement date. The daily files count short sales as they trade, including those bought back within hours. In the 11 sessions from August 14 to 28, whose trades settled by August 31, the files counted 41.9 million shares marked short, while short interest rose by 1.04 million. In the ten sessions from August 31 to September 14 they counted 11.6 million, while short interest rose by 347,465.</p>\n<h2 id=\"daily-prices-suggest-the-short-sale-price-test-covered-more-than-half-of-usdes-sessions\">Daily prices suggest the short sale price test covered more than half of USDE's sessions</h2>\n<p>The SEC's short sale price test, Rule 201, applies once a stock falls 10% or more below the prior day's regular-hours close, for the rest of that day and all of the next, according to the SEC staff's <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/rules-regulations/staff-guidance/trading-markets-frequently-asked-questions-7\">answers on the rule</a>. While it applies, a short sale may not be executed or displayed at or below the national best bid, the highest price any market is bidding, unless it meets one of the rule's exceptions. A firm marks a sale that relies on one short exempt. The listing market decides when the test is triggered, and this review did not read Nasdaq's record of it.</p>\n<p>By S&amp;P Global's daily lows, USDE fell 10% or more below its prior close on 25 of its 73 sessions, implying the test would have applied on 39, or 38 if no prior close applied on June 26. Those 39 sessions carried 88.4% of the 6.97 million shares that FINRA's files counted as short exempt, and July 2 alone carried 39.6%. The SEC staff's guidance lets firms mark some sales short exempt on days without the test too, and the other 11.6% came on 25 of the 34 sessions without it.</p>\n<p>S&amp;P Global's history for USDE includes a $9.40 close on June 25 on 145 shares, the day before USDE's first session, which appears to be the last close of TLGY Acquisition Corp., the listed blank-check company in StablecoinX's merger that day. A blank-check company is a listed shell formed to merge with a private business. The count uses that close as the prior close for USDE's first session. The SEC staff's guidance says the rule does not apply on the first day of trading of a newly offered security, which has no prior close, and whether Nasdaq treated USDE that way is not known.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-this-review-could-not-check\">What this review could not check</h2>\n<p>FINRA's figures do not say who held the short positions, why, or what borrowing the shares cost. Some short sales may hedge other positions, such as the warrants, and the data cannot show that.</p>\n<p>The September 30 figures were published on October 9 and are the latest settlement snapshot included here. They do not establish the short position at the October 8 close. Each record flags a revision of the figure before it. None of the seven records read flagged one.</p>\n<p>FINRA's daily files leave out trades reported outside regular hours, so the 41.2% ratio is not a measure of all off-exchange trading in USDE. The price test count rests on S&amp;P Global's daily lows. For the 31 sessions from August 26 each was within a cent of the lowest regular-session price in Yahoo Finance's hourly data, and earlier sessions had no second source. Moving any session's low by a cent would add at most one session to the count.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reproduce-the-numbers\">Reproduce the numbers</h2>\n<p>This review read FINRA's short interest records from its public Query API and its daily files from FINRA's public file server on October 9, 2026 UTC, with the SHA-256 of each response. The main script checks every input against its recorded hash, that each record's previous position matches the record before it, that FINRA's files hold one USDE row for each session, and that the price test logic marks the day after each trigger. If any check fails, the script stops before writing its tables. Download the <a download href=\"/research-assets/stablecoinx-short-interest-2026-10-08/short_interest.csv\">short interest table</a>, the <a download href=\"/research-assets/stablecoinx-short-interest-2026-10-08/daily_short_volume.csv\">daily short sale volume</a>, or the <a download href=\"/research-assets/stablecoinx-short-interest-2026-10-08/calculation-files.zip\">calculation files and source inputs</a>.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">share of freely tradable = short interest ÷ 19,063,653 freely tradable shares\n                         = 2,810,986 ÷ 19,063,653 = 14.75% on September 30\ndays to cover            = short interest ÷ average daily volume over the cycle\n                         = 2,810,986 ÷ 6,964,553 = 0.40\nshare marked short       = short volume ÷ volume reported to FINRA\n                         = 90,743,186 ÷ 156,995,182 = 57.8%\nprice test triggered     = daily low ≤ 0.9 × prior close\n</code></pre>\n<p>The <a href=\"/stablecoinx/\">StablecoinX dashboard</a> tracks USDE's share price against the ENA treasury the company reports, along with its warrants and options, and its <a href=\"/methodology/\">methodology page</a> defines its figures. The <a href=\"/research/usde-september-2026/\">September review</a> breaks down what moved USDE that month.</p>\n<p>Analysis of public market data. Not investment advice.</p>\n<p>Related research: <a href=\"/research/stablecoinx-float-2026-10-08/\">The float and ownership review explains the share-count denominators.</a></p><div class=\"article-next\"><span>Continue exploring</span><a href=\"/stablecoinx/\">Open the StablecoinX dashboard →</a><a href=\"/research/\">All research and guides →</a></div></article>";
